2026-06-30 15:49:55,438 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-b192b24a-3e7c-42bc-a47e-5ed40b679cfa', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\n20210831_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0120C_54_p62_Baf_01.raw\n20210831_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0120C_54_p62_Baf_02.raw\n20210831_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0120C_54_p62_Baf_03.raw\n20210831_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0120C_54_p62_DMSO_01.raw\n20210831_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0120C_54_p62_DMSO_02.raw\n20210831_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0120C_54_p62_DMSO_03.raw\n20210831_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0120C_WT_p62_Baf_01.raw\n20210831_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0120C_WT_p62_Baf_02.raw\n20210831_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0120C_WT_p62_Baf_03.raw\n20210831_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0120C_WT_p62_DMSO_01.raw\n20210831_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0120C_WT_p62_DMSO_02.raw\n20210831_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0120C_WT_p62_DMSO_03.raw\n20220626_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0159_CO_01.raw\n20220626_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0159_CO_02.raw\n20220626_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0159_CO_03.raw\n20220626_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0159_p62_HCT116_133_BAF_01.raw\n20220626_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0159_p62_HCT116_133_BAF_02.raw\n20220626_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0159_p62_HCT116_133_BAF_03.raw\n20220626_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0159_p62_HCT116_133_DMSO_01.raw\n20220626_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0159_p62_HCT116_133_DMSO_02.raw\n20220626_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0159_p62_HCT116_133_DMSO_03.raw\n20241108_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0245B_1.raw\n20241108_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0245B_10.raw\n20241108_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0245B_11.raw\n20241108_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0245B_12.raw\n20241108_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0245B_13.raw\n20241108_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0245B_14.raw\n20241108_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0245B_15.raw\n20241108_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0245B_16.raw\n20241108_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0245B_2.raw\n20241108_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0245B_3.raw\n20241108_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0245B_4.raw\n20241108_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0245B_5.raw\n20241108_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0245B_6.raw\n20241108_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0245B_7.raw\n20241108_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0245B_8.raw\n20241108_HF1_MaRa_SA_P0245B_9.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nDataset 3: APEX2-p62 autophagosome lumen profiling proteomics\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nAberrant chromosomal content, or aneuploidy, profoundly affects cellular physiology. Even a gain of a single chromosome disrupts proteostasis due to overexpression of numerous proteins. Consequently, cells accumulate SQSTM1/p62-positive cytosolic bodies and show altered proteasomal and lysosomal activity. To elucidate the p62 interaction network in aneuploid cells, we conducted p62 immunoprecipitation and proximity labeling assays followed by mass spectrometry analysis. Our investigation revealed the enrichment of mitochondrial proteins within the cytosolic p62 interactome and proximome in aneuploid cells, but not in the proximome spatially confined to autophagosomes. Immunofluorescence confirmed increased colocalization of p62 with novel interactors and with mitochondrial proteins in polysomic cells. Moreover, we observed mitochondrial damage characterized by increased perinuclear clustering, reduced oxygen consumption, and reduced mitochondrial DNA abundance in polysomic cells. Furthermore, we demonstrate that polysomic cells exhibit reduced import of mitochondrial proteins and accumulation of mitochondria precursor proteins in the cytosol. Our data suggest that proteotoxic stress induced by chromosome gains leads to the sequestration of mitochondrial precursor proteins into cytosolic p62-bodies, thereby compromising mitochondrial function.\n\n[METHODS]\nAnalysis of p62 cargo candidates engulfed in autophagosomes by proximity biotinylation-based quantitative proteomics Autophagosomal content profiling for p62 cargo candidates was performed as described by {Zellner, 2021 #17}. Briefly, APEX2-mediated biotin labeled cells were incubated on an overhead rotator for 20 min at 4 oC in homogenization buffer I (10 mM KCl, 1.5mM MgCl2, 10 mM HEPES-KOH and 1mM DTT pH 7.5). Cells were then lysed by dounce homogenization and mixed at a ratio of 1:5 with homogenization buffer II (375 mM KCl, 22.5 mM MgCl2, 220 mM HEPES-KOH and 0.5 mM DTT pH 7.5). Cleared lysates obtained by centrifugation at 600 g for 10 min were treated with 100 mg/ml Proteinase K (PK) and 1 mM CaCl2 for 1 h at 37 oC, before inhibition of PK activity by adding 10 mM PMSF to the samples. To account for Proteinase K resistant proteins, samples additionally treated with 0.1 % RAPIGest (RP) were added as control. Samples were centrifuged at 17000 g for 15 min at 4 oC. The supernatant was removed, and pellets were resuspended in RIPA buffer containing quenching components, followed by brief sonication and centrifugation at 10000 g for 10 min h at 4 oC. Supernatants were then incubated on pre-equilibrated Streptavidin-Agarose (Sigma) overnight at 4 oC. Samples were then washed three times in RIPA buffer containing quenching components and three times in 3 M Urea buffer (prepared with 50 mM NH4HCO3 solution). Subsequently, samples were incubated with 5 mM TCEP for 30 min at 55 oC and alkylated with 10 mM IAA for 20 min at RT. Alkylation was quenched by the addition of 20 mM DTT and samples were washed twice with 2 M Urea buffer (prepared with 50 mM NH4HCO3 solution) and digested overnight at 37 oC using 1 µg of trypsin per 20 µl beads. Digested peptides from the supernatants were collected and pooled together with supernatants from two times washes of the beads using 2M Urea buffer. The pooled samples were then acidified with 1 % TFA and concentrated by vacuum centrifugation. Finally, the digested peptides were desalted and purified on C18 stage tips for analysis by mass spectrometry. To validate the PK protection assay by Western blot, the cleared lysates obtained by centrifugation at 600 g for 10 min were treated with 30 mg/ml PK and 1 mM CaCl2 and incubated for 30 min at 37 oC. Control reactions consisting of no digestion, digestions with PK and 0.1% RP or treatment with 0.1 % RP only were included. Samples were then boiled in Laemmli buffer and subjected to SDS-PAGE. Liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry Peptides were resuspended in 9:1 mixture of buffer A (0.1% formic acid) and buffer A* (2% acetonitrile, 0.1% trifluoro acetic acid). Half of the sample was separated on 50\u2009cm columns packed in house with ReproSil‐Pur C18‐AQ 1.9\u2009μm resin (Dr. Maisch GmbH). Using an EASY‐nLC 1200 ultra‐high-pressure system connected in-line to a Q-Exactive HF Mass Spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific), liquid chromatography was conducted. Peptides were introduced in buffer A (0.1% formic acid) and eluted with a non-linear gradient of 5–60% buffer B (0.1% formic acid, 80% acetonitrile) at a rate of 300\u2009nl/min over 90\u2009min. Column temperature was maintained at 60\u2009°C. Data acquisition alternated between a full scan (60\u2009K resolution, maximum injection time of 20\u2009ms, AGC target of 3e6) and 15 data‐dependent MS/MS scans (15\u2009K resolution, maximum injection time of 80\u2009ms, AGC target of 1.6e3). The isolation window was 1.4 m/z, and normalized collision energy was 28. Dynamic exclusion was set to 30\u2009seconds.  TMT-labelled peptides were separated as described above except that a 180 min gradient was used for elution. MS data was acquired in data-dependent mode using the following settings. Full MS scans (120\u2009K resolution, maximum injection time of 80\u2009ms, AGC target of 3e6) were alternated with a set of maximally 15 data‐dependent MS/MS scans (60\u2009K resolution, maximum injection time of 100\u2009ms, AGC target of 2.0e3). The isolation window was 0.7 m/z, and normalized collision energy was 32. Fixed first mass was set to 100 m/z. Dynamic exclusion was set to 30\u2009seconds.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
