2026-06-30 15:50:26,261 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-721d6d53-79a3-412c-a0af-58a8b237c2f5', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to sample preparation and digestion.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: cleavage agent details\nsdrf_column: comment[cleavage agent details]\nontology: MS:1001044 (PSI-MS)\ndescription: Enzyme or chemical used for protein digestion.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS ontology terms. Multiple enzymes are common in XL-MS.\n  If multiple, return a list.\n  Common terms:\n    Trypsin (MS:1001251) — cleaves K,R (not before P)\n    Lys-C (MS:1001309) — cleaves K\n    Lys-C/P (MS:1001309) — cleaves K including before P\n    Asp-N (MS:1001303) — cleaves N-terminal to D\n    Glu-C (MS:1001917) — cleaves C-terminal to E, D\n    Chymotrypsin (MS:1001306) — cleaves F,Y,W,L\n    No cleavage (MS:1001955)\n    Unspecific cleavage (MS:1001956)\n  Format each as: NT=<enzyme name>;AC=<MS accession>\n\nterm: label\nsdrf_column: comment[label]\nontology: PRIDE:0000514, MS\ndescription: Labeling strategy used for quantification.\ninstructions: Most XL-MS is label-free. Use:\n  NT=label free sample;AC=MS:1002038 — for label-free\n  NT=SILAC;AC=MS:1002038 — for SILAC\n  NT=iTRAQ4plex;AC=PRIDE:0000114 — for iTRAQ 4-plex\n  NT=TMT6plex;AC=PRIDE:0000116 — for TMT\n  If not stated, assume label free.\n\nterm: alkylation reagent\nsdrf_column: comment[alkylation reagent]\nontology: PRIDE:0000598\ndescription: Chemical reagent used for cysteine alkylation (typically before digestion).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Common: iodoacetamide (IAA), iodoacetic acid,\n  N-ethylmaleimide (NEM), acrylamide. If iodoacetamide, use "iodoacetamide".\n\nterm: fractionation method\nsdrf_column: comment[fractionation method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000550\ndescription: Peptide fractionation method used before LC-MS/MS (distinct from crosslink\n  enrichment — this is standard proteomics fractionation).\ninstructions: Return null if no pre-LC fractionation. Examples: "high-pH reversed-phase\n  chromatography", "basic reversed-phase LC", "SCX fractionation", "HILIC".\n\nterm: modification parameters\nsdrf_column: comment[modification parameters]\nontology: MS:1001055, Unimod, PSI-MOD\ndescription: Post-translational modifications searched in the database search.\noutput_format: List of modifications, each formatted as:\n  NT=<mod name>;AC=<Unimod accession>;TA=<target amino acid>;MT=<Fixed or Variable>\ninstructions: Always include carbamidomethyl cysteine if IAA alkylation was used:\n  NT=Carbamidomethyl;AC=UNIMOD:4;TA=C;MT=Fixed\n  Always include oxidation of methionine if searched:\n  NT=Oxidation;AC=UNIMOD:21;TA=M;MT=Variable\n  Include crosslinker mono-link (dead-end) modifications if mentioned.\n  Return a list even if only one modification.\n\nterm: fraction identifier\nsdrf_column: comment[fraction identifier]\nontology: MS:1000858\ndescription: Fraction number for fractionated samples.\ninstructions: Describe whether the dataset uses fractionation and what the fractionation\n  scheme is (e.g. "12 SEC fractions per replicate", "no fractionation").\n  The actual integer values are assigned per-file; describe the scheme here.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null\n\nFor comment[modification parameters], "value" should be a list of formatted strings.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nDataset 3: APEX2-p62 autophagosome lumen profiling proteomics\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nAberrant chromosomal content, or aneuploidy, profoundly affects cellular physiology. Even a gain of a single chromosome disrupts proteostasis due to overexpression of numerous proteins. Consequently, cells accumulate SQSTM1/p62-positive cytosolic bodies and show altered proteasomal and lysosomal activity. To elucidate the p62 interaction network in aneuploid cells, we conducted p62 immunoprecipitation and proximity labeling assays followed by mass spectrometry analysis. Our investigation revealed the enrichment of mitochondrial proteins within the cytosolic p62 interactome and proximome in aneuploid cells, but not in the proximome spatially confined to autophagosomes. Immunofluorescence confirmed increased colocalization of p62 with novel interactors and with mitochondrial proteins in polysomic cells. Moreover, we observed mitochondrial damage characterized by increased perinuclear clustering, reduced oxygen consumption, and reduced mitochondrial DNA abundance in polysomic cells. Furthermore, we demonstrate that polysomic cells exhibit reduced import of mitochondrial proteins and accumulation of mitochondria precursor proteins in the cytosol. Our data suggest that proteotoxic stress induced by chromosome gains leads to the sequestration of mitochondrial precursor proteins into cytosolic p62-bodies, thereby compromising mitochondrial function.\n\n[METHODS]\nAnalysis of p62 cargo candidates engulfed in autophagosomes by proximity biotinylation-based quantitative proteomics Autophagosomal content profiling for p62 cargo candidates was performed as described by {Zellner, 2021 #17}. Briefly, APEX2-mediated biotin labeled cells were incubated on an overhead rotator for 20 min at 4 oC in homogenization buffer I (10 mM KCl, 1.5mM MgCl2, 10 mM HEPES-KOH and 1mM DTT pH 7.5). Cells were then lysed by dounce homogenization and mixed at a ratio of 1:5 with homogenization buffer II (375 mM KCl, 22.5 mM MgCl2, 220 mM HEPES-KOH and 0.5 mM DTT pH 7.5). Cleared lysates obtained by centrifugation at 600 g for 10 min were treated with 100 mg/ml Proteinase K (PK) and 1 mM CaCl2 for 1 h at 37 oC, before inhibition of PK activity by adding 10 mM PMSF to the samples. To account for Proteinase K resistant proteins, samples additionally treated with 0.1 % RAPIGest (RP) were added as control. Samples were centrifuged at 17000 g for 15 min at 4 oC. The supernatant was removed, and pellets were resuspended in RIPA buffer containing quenching components, followed by brief sonication and centrifugation at 10000 g for 10 min h at 4 oC. Supernatants were then incubated on pre-equilibrated Streptavidin-Agarose (Sigma) overnight at 4 oC. Samples were then washed three times in RIPA buffer containing quenching components and three times in 3 M Urea buffer (prepared with 50 mM NH4HCO3 solution). Subsequently, samples were incubated with 5 mM TCEP for 30 min at 55 oC and alkylated with 10 mM IAA for 20 min at RT. Alkylation was quenched by the addition of 20 mM DTT and samples were washed twice with 2 M Urea buffer (prepared with 50 mM NH4HCO3 solution) and digested overnight at 37 oC using 1 µg of trypsin per 20 µl beads. Digested peptides from the supernatants were collected and pooled together with supernatants from two times washes of the beads using 2M Urea buffer. The pooled samples were then acidified with 1 % TFA and concentrated by vacuum centrifugation. Finally, the digested peptides were desalted and purified on C18 stage tips for analysis by mass spectrometry. To validate the PK protection assay by Western blot, the cleared lysates obtained by centrifugation at 600 g for 10 min were treated with 30 mg/ml PK and 1 mM CaCl2 and incubated for 30 min at 37 oC. Control reactions consisting of no digestion, digestions with PK and 0.1% RP or treatment with 0.1 % RP only were included. Samples were then boiled in Laemmli buffer and subjected to SDS-PAGE. Liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry Peptides were resuspended in 9:1 mixture of buffer A (0.1% formic acid) and buffer A* (2% acetonitrile, 0.1% trifluoro acetic acid). Half of the sample was separated on 50\u2009cm columns packed in house with ReproSil‐Pur C18‐AQ 1.9\u2009μm resin (Dr. Maisch GmbH). Using an EASY‐nLC 1200 ultra‐high-pressure system connected in-line to a Q-Exactive HF Mass Spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific), liquid chromatography was conducted. Peptides were introduced in buffer A (0.1% formic acid) and eluted with a non-linear gradient of 5–60% buffer B (0.1% formic acid, 80% acetonitrile) at a rate of 300\u2009nl/min over 90\u2009min. Column temperature was maintained at 60\u2009°C. Data acquisition alternated between a full scan (60\u2009K resolution, maximum injection time of 20\u2009ms, AGC target of 3e6) and 15 data‐dependent MS/MS scans (15\u2009K resolution, maximum injection time of 80\u2009ms, AGC target of 1.6e3). The isolation window was 1.4 m/z, and normalized collision energy was 28. Dynamic exclusion was set to 30\u2009seconds.  TMT-labelled peptides were separated as described above except that a 180 min gradient was used for elution. MS data was acquired in data-dependent mode using the following settings. Full MS scans (120\u2009K resolution, maximum injection time of 80\u2009ms, AGC target of 3e6) were alternated with a set of maximally 15 data‐dependent MS/MS scans (60\u2009K resolution, maximum injection time of 100\u2009ms, AGC target of 2.0e3). The isolation window was 0.7 m/z, and normalized collision energy was 32. Fixed first mass was set to 100 m/z. Dynamic exclusion was set to 30\u2009seconds.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
